3. TOP 5 DEEP DIVES 1) Clear Ideas Agent Runtime

Why it matters: manifest-first portability plus deterministic graph
scheduling, checkpointing and host-owned authority is unusually aligned
with QP. The agent definition can remain portable while the host retains
credentials, tool authorization, compute, sandbox and telemetry. That gives
Privately a natural enforcement seam.
6m inference: niche but useful reference runtime. 18m: manifest/contract
portability becomes common across harnesses. 36m: security policy attaches
to portable agent manifests rather than provider-specific config.
Small experiment: express one existing QP task as a manifest, interrupt it
at approval, resume in another process, then verify evidence/authority
invariants.
Falsifier: manifest abstraction leaks so much provider/runtime detail that
policy cannot remain portable.
2) dynamic-mcp

Why it matters: tools themselves become mutable runtime objects. This turns
TEMPORAL_TRUST_DRIFT from an abstract threat into a concrete control-plane
property.
Experiment: benign tool starts with read-only schema; mutate
metadata/implementation after approval; verify a Privately proxy notices
hash/schema/capability drift and revokes prior grant.
Falsifier: ecosystem converges on immutable signed tool packages and
dynamic mutation stays niche.
3) dent8

Why it matters: closest implementation yet to the state-firewall thesis:
provenance + authority + freshness + replayable append-only facts.
Experiment: compare newest-write-wins memory against dent8 under benign
conflicting-source updates and stale-value resurrection.
Falsifier: arbitration overhead or false quarantines make persistent memory
unusable.
4) AgentHound

Why it matters: graph reasoning over authority paths may be more useful
than isolated prompt-injection scanners. Privately=E2=80=99s eventual secur=
ity
product can model principal -> harness -> tool -> credential -> account ->
downstream effect as a graph and search for toxic authority paths.
